"Functional interface" is just a term meaning an interface that has exactly one abstract method.
 They are not new, but the need to distinguish them is new in Java 8.

Functional interfaces are significant because they are the only types that can serve as the context for a lambda expression
or method reference in Java 8. A lambda expression is a simplified syntax that allows you to define an "anonymous function",
 and it will evaluate to an object where that "anonymous function" is the implementation of some method in that object,
 without specifying the name of the method. A type having exactly one abstract method is necessary to make it unambiguous
 which method the body of the lambda is implementing.

